      body: "Both arrays and List<T>, covered elsewhere in this course, support the same two loop styles: a for loop indexing in with [i] when the position itself matters, or a foreach loop when it doesn't. A for loop over a List<int> scores looks like for (int i = 0; i < scores.Count; i++), using .Count instead of an array's .Length, but otherwise behaving identically - both give you index-based access to every element in order. Mixing the two styles inside one method is common and genuinely useful: a for loop when you need to know which position you're at, a foreach loop for a simpler pass where the position doesn't matter, both operating on the exact same underlying collection.",
      altExplain: "Arrays and Lists both loop the same two ways - a for loop with an index when position matters, foreach when it doesn't. Lists use .Count where arrays use .Length.",
