      body: "Sometimes a base class shouldn't ever be created directly - there's no such thing as a generic Shape on its own, only specific shapes like circles and squares that each know how to compute their own area. An abstract class marks exactly this: abstract class Shape can hold real fields and real methods like any other class, but it can also declare abstract methods, like public abstract double GetArea();, that have no body at all and must be implemented by every class that inherits from it. The compiler enforces both halves of this: you can never write new Shape() directly, and any concrete class inheriting from Shape must override every one of its abstract methods, or that class has to be marked abstract too.",
