      title: "Abstract classes vs interfaces",
      body: "Both abstract classes and interfaces let you require that derived classes implement certain methods, which makes it easy to reach for either one without thinking hard about which actually fits. The deciding question is usually: does this relationship share real state or behavior, or is it just a promise of capability? An abstract class is the right choice when derived classes genuinely share fields, a constructor, or real implemented methods alongside the parts that must be overridden - Shape's Describe method, reused by every derived shape, is exactly this. An interface is the right choice when you're only guaranteeing a capability with no shared implementation at all, especially when that capability needs to apply across classes that aren't otherwise related - IFlyable can apply to a Duck and a Plane despite those having nothing else in common, which a shared abstract base class would awkwardly force together.",
