      body: "An instance member (a field or method without the static keyword) belongs to a specific object - each object gets its own copy. A static member instead belongs to the class itself, shared across every object of that class, and is accessed through the class name rather than an object variable, like Math.PI or Math.Sqrt(). Static members are useful for values or behavior that don't depend on any particular object's individual state - a counter tracking how many objects have been created in total is a classic example, since that count belongs to the class as a whole, not to any single instance, and a static method like a small math helper doesn't need an object to operate on at all.",
